+sweep_case catches 'Ten of the twenty-one confirmed findings in the last release were defects'
+sweep_case catches 'Nine of the fourteen blocking findings'
+sweep_case catches '10 of the 21 confirmed findings in the last release were defects'
+sweep_case catches '10 of 21 confirmed findings were defects in the gates'
+sweep_case catches '21 confirmed findings'
+sweep_case catches '116 findings'
+# The qualifier between the numeral and the noun is arbitrary. These are enumerated from the DEFECT
+# (a numeral quantifying `findings`), not from the adjectives a particular escape happened to use,
+# which is how a closed four-word list gets arrived at.
+sweep_case catches 'Ten of the twenty-one confirmed severe findings were defects'
+sweep_case catches 'twenty-one distinct findings'
+sweep_case catches '21 of the findings were defects'
+# The unit is either spelling too. "47% more confirmed findings" and "47 percent more confirmed
+# findings" are one published figure, and a sweep that reads only the symbol admits the second.
+sweep_case catches 'a 47 percent uplift'
+sweep_case catches 'the second reviewer added 47 percent more confirmed findings'
+sweep_case catches 'a forty-seven percent uplift'
+# The word list has to be CLOSED, not merely long, so there is one case per token, and the token lists
+# below are LITERAL — deliberately not spelled `$COUNTNUM`. A table generated from the enumeration it
+# exists to pin goes green the moment a token is deleted, because the case is deleted with it.
+# Hand-picked sentences do not scale here either: a single case for `sixty` reading `Sixty of the
+# ninety confirmed findings were defects` still matches on `ninety` once `sixty` is removed, and a
+# token nobody thought to write a sentence for — `million`, `billion` — gets no case at all. One
